// Package cache is what a generated file says it was generated from.
//
// Some of the SNGL a build reads is written by something other than a person:
// the widget declarations derived from a GTK install's introspection data, the
// value a `#[foreign(pure)]` function returned when the compiler ran it. Each
// is the output of a *producer* -- a step that reads the world outside the
// compiler, often by running another process -- and each is stored between
// builds, so that a build whose inputs have not changed does not run the step
// again.
//
// The file carries its own answer to "is this still what the producer would
// write". A `cache.inputs` directive at its root lists every input the
// producer read, each with the value it had, and a store holding the file
// re-checks each one before handing the file back:
//
//     cache.inputs {
//         cache.file(path="/usr/share/gir-1.0/Gtk-4.0.gir", sha256="9f86d0…")
//         cache.absent(path="/usr/local/share/gir-1.0/Gtk-4.0.gir")
//     }
//
// The store is separate from the file on purpose. The file says what it
// depends on; where a previous answer is kept, how much of them is kept and
// for how long is the store's business, and a file written by hand into a
// project reads the same way.
//
// The inputs are recorded before the producer runs, so a file edited while it
// runs is recorded as it was before the edit and is found stale next time --
// the order that makes a race cost a repeat rather than a wrong answer.
//
// An input a producer did not record is an input the store cannot see, which
// for a value folded into a build is a wrong build rather than a slow one.
// That is why a pure function may not read a file or the environment while it
// runs: nothing records what it read.
